## What I changed

`src/main.rs` — one door-aware refusal builder, called from both non-interactive prompt doors:

• Added enum PromptDoor { Piped, SinglePrompt } and the pure slash_command_refusal(input, door) -> Option<String> next to looks_like_slash_command. It returns None when the predicate is false — the predicate is unchanged and stays the single authority for which inputs are refused, so the two doors' agreement is a property of the code, not of a test.
• The Piped arm is byte-identical to the five inline eprintln! lines it replaced; run_piped_mode now does if let Some(msg) = ... { eprint!("{msg}"); exit(2) }.
• The SinglePrompt arm names the -p spelling, offers only working doors (yoyo, yoyo doctor), states the refusal lands on stderr with exit code 2 and no API call, and names the boundary. It never suggests --prompt.
• The refusal sits at the dispatch site before run_single_prompt, so both the image and text branches are covered and no agent is built for a refused prompt. Exit code 2 on both doors.

`src/main_tests.rs` — 4 tests: a table over both doors (Some for /risk,   /risk, /risk now; None for explain risk, fix src/main.rs, a /risk sentence, "", with an explicit doors-agree assertion), a byte-identity pin on the piped arm, the --prompt trap with a non-vacuous companion, and a deliberately weak source-level guard counting >= 3 references (definition + two call sites — >= 2 would be satisfied by the definition plus one door, the exact defect).

`docs/src/usage/single-prompt.md` — the new section on slash-command refusal.

`ARCHITECTURE.md` — the src/main.rs entry. I did not invent the issue number the brief hinted at; I also recorded residue (a) as a superseded claim: the brief said piped mode's message "is pinned by pre-existing tests", and git grep at HEAD returns exactly one hit — the inline eprintln! itself, with no test referencing it. So that pin is newly earned by this task, not pre-existing.

